<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    The base entitlements. `scripts/build.sh` copies this file to
    `entitlements.signed.plist` and adds the `keychain-access-groups` entry
    that the release build actually signs with; that entry needs the signing
    team's ID, which is not in the repository. `tauri dev` and any build that
    does not go through build.sh use this file as-is, get no keychain group,
    and fall back to the login keychain — see `aka_core::keychain`.

    The bundled Node runs the sidecar, and V8 cannot allocate executable
    memory for its JIT under the hardened runtime without these. Without
    them a signed `node` dies with SIGTRAP the moment it starts.

    Tauri applies one entitlements file to every binary it signs in the
    bundle, so the main executable receives these too. That is broader than
    ideal — only `node` needs them, and with the keychain group added at
    sign time the bundled `node` is also nominally in the group that can
    read vault items. Narrowing it means re-signing `node` separately after
    Tauri's pass and then re-sealing the bundle, which is worth doing if the
    app is ever audited against a stricter baseline.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
